Subject: [#94-5-94] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage

[My extropians subscription seems to be going through another
unreliable phase, where a large fraction of messages just never get
here.  (For example, Tim's is the only response to my post on this
topic I've seen, and though the stats say Minsky is posting lots, I've
nevery actually seen a post of his.)  So if you want to be sure I see
something, please cc me directly.  RH]

Tim Starr writes:
>>For example, if one imagines that each human had enough
>>(non-human-capital) wealth to buy a robot slave on average 100 times
>>as productive as that human (ranging say from 80x on some tasks to
>>1000x on others), they it is likely that few people would work except
>>for the joy of work.
>
>I'm afraid I'm back to disagreeing with you on this one, Robin.  Unless
>the robot is omnitasking, I'd assign it to the most financially
>rewarding task(s) at any given time and engage my own labor in the next-
>most personally rewarding task - such personal rewards could include
>financial remuneration.  The law of comparative advantage still holds
>here.  I'm better off doing something the roboslave's infinitely better
>at in conjunction with it doing something else it's infinitely more
>productive at than I than with me doing nothing while the roboslave
>engages in its production.  And, since I'd be competing with others,
>I'd have their production to stimulate mine.

I wasn't thinking of you doing "nothing" so much as engaging in
"leisure".  Leisure can be very "productive" toward your personal
utility, but is usually distinguished from "work".  There would of
course be exceptions, but models that predict how much leisure folks
take now also predict folks take more leisure as they get more "slave"
equivalents.

Robin Hanson

